A CALL'S FORMS COME FROM THE HOST, not from a list anybody typed here. TiddlyWiki decides them in
`parseMacroInvocationAsTransclusion` and `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute`, and every line below stands
verified against that parser: the call it builds, and the TYPE it binds each value.

! The call itself

A name alone: <<aMacro>> here.

The name admits anything but whitespace, `>`, a quote, `=` or `:` — so <<.def A definition macro>>
calls `.def` with three positional values.

A NAME STOPS AT ITS FIRST `:`, so <<$:/a/system/name>> calls a macro named `$` and binds
`:/a/system/name` as a positional value — never the system title a reader sees.

A NAME STOPS AT `=`, and what follows binds as a value rather than as part of the name:
<<a=b>> builds a call on `a`.

A closer may stand lines below its opener, which no single-line pattern can see:

<<aMacro
  one="a value"
>>

! Positional values, which every separator admits

<<aMacro one two>>

<<aMacro "a double quoted value">>

<<aMacro 'a single quoted value'>>

<<aMacro """a triple quoted value""">>

<<aMacro [[a bracketed value]]>>

An unquoted value carries a lone `>`, because the closer needs two: <<aMacro a>b>> here.

Separators inside a value bind nothing: <<mymacro one, two; three>> stands as three values.

! Named values — `=` ADMITS EVERY FORM

<<aMacro one=unquoted>>

<<aMacro one="a double quoted value">>

<<aMacro one='a single quoted value'>>

<<aMacro one="""a triple quoted value""">>

<<aMacro one=[[a bracketed value]]>>

A filtered value, which the host types `filtered`: <<aMacro one={{{ [tag[Done]sort[title]] }}}>>

An indirect value, typed `indirect`: <<aMacro one={{SomeTiddler}}>>

A call as a value, typed `macro`: <<aMacro one=<<inner>>>>

A multi-valued reference, also typed `macro`: <<aMacro one=((aList))>>

A substituted value, typed `substituted`: <<aMacro one=`a $(variable)$ value`>>

And its fenced spelling: <<aMacro one=```a substituted value```>>

! Named values — `:` ADMITS ONLY A STRING

The old separator takes a quoted or bracketed value and nothing else:

<<aMacro one:"a value">>

<<aMacro one:"""a triple quoted value""">>

A NEW-STYLE VALUE AFTER `:` FRAGMENTS. The host refuses the filtered form here and reads three
values instead of one — `one`, then the filter's halves as positionals:

<<aMacro one:{{{ [tag[x]] }}}>>

A colon needs a strict identifier before it, so a system title stays one positional value rather
than a name and a value: <<aMacro $:/foo>> here.

! What the host refuses

`<<` MUST BE FOLLOWED IMMEDIATELY BY THE NAME. The name regexp reads from the angle brackets' own
end, so padding opens nothing and this line stands as prose:

<<  aMacro  one=1  >>

A call with no name builds nothing: << >> stands as prose.

An opener with no closer anywhere ahead builds nothing either, and `degenerate.unterminated.tw`
carries that shape, because a carrier holding it bleeds past its own end by design.

! The substitution forms a definition's body carries

$(aVariable)$

$aParameter$

${ [tag[x]] }$
